course: biology
lesson: 225
title: Caring for a pet: needs, dangers and responsibility
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: When a family brings a dog home from a rescue centre, everyone is excited, but the dog is now counting on them every single day for years, not only while it is new and fun.
explain: Every pet has five needs: a good home, the right food and fresh water, the chance to behave like its kind, company or the right to live alone if that suits it, and protection from pain and illness.
explain: Some of our everyday foods and plants are dangerous to pets: chocolate to dogs, grapes and raisins to dogs, and lilies to cats, where even a little pollen licked off the fur can harm the kidneys.
explain: A tiny microchip under the skin helps a lost pet get back home, walks and play keep it fit and happy, and a clean cage keeps it from getting ill.
explain: That is why people say a pet is for life, not just for Christmas: a cute puppy becomes a big dog that needs walks, food and love for ten years or more.
fact: Rescue centres care for animals that are lost or given up, and these animals need new homes.
fact: Every pet has five welfare needs: a suitable home, the right food and water, to behave normally, company or the right to be alone, and protection from pain and illness.
fact: Pets need proper food made for their kind; rich human food, like a cat eating lasagne, can make them ill.
fact: Chocolate is poisonous to dogs, because it contains a chemical that dogs cannot break down well.
fact: Lilies are very poisonous to cats; even pollen licked off their fur can damage the kidneys.
fact: Grapes and raisins can damage dogs' kidneys, so dropped grapes should be picked up at once.
fact: A microchip is a tiny chip under a pet's skin that holds a number linked to the owner, so a lost pet can be returned.
fact: Cages and hutches need cleaning regularly; dirty bedding left for a month causes smells and illness.
fact: When a family goes on holiday, a pet sitter, kennels or a friend must feed and care for the pet.
fact: Dogs need exercise every day; walks keep them fit and happy.
fact: Play keeps pets happy; hunting games with toys keep a cat's mind busy.
fact: A pet is for life: a puppy given as a Christmas present grows up and needs many years of care.
fact: If you think a pet has eaten something poisonous, tell an adult at once so they can call a vet.
exercise: Why do animals at a rescue centre need new homes? | They were lost or given up and need someone to care for them | They are wild animals caught in forests | They are too old to ever be adopted | They are sick and cannot live with people
exercise: Which of these is one of a pet's five welfare needs? | Protection from pain and illness | A new toy every single day | Its own room with a television | Sweets as a daily treat
exercise: Why should a dog never be given chocolate as a treat? | It contains a chemical that is poisonous to dogs | It makes dogs' fur fall out in big patches | Dogs hate the taste of sweet things | It makes dogs forget their names
exercise: A cat chews a lily from a vase. Why is that serious? | Lilies are very poisonous to cats and harm their kidneys | Lilies make cats sneeze for a day | Lily petals get stuck in cats' teeth and are hard to get out | Lilies make cats sleep too long
exercise: How can a lily harm a cat that never eats the flower? | It can lick poisonous pollen off its fur | The smell alone kills cats at once | Cats are scared of lilies and run away into the road | Lily leaves scratch cats' eyes
exercise: Grapes fall on the kitchen floor near the dog. What should you do? | Pick them up quickly; they can harm a dog's kidneys | Leave them; grapes are healthy fruit for dogs | Let the dog eat only the green ones | Give the dog the raisins instead, because dried grapes are safe
exercise: What does a pet's microchip do? | It holds a number that links the pet to its owner | It tracks the pet like a map on a phone | It stops the pet from getting ill | It lets the pet open the cat flap and nothing else at all
exercise: How often should a hamster's cage be cleaned? | Regularly, not just once a month | Never, hamsters like dirty bedding | Just once a year, early in the spring | Only on the days when the hamster is ill
exercise: A family is going on holiday for a week. What must happen to their cat? | Someone like a pet sitter or friend must care for it | Leave a week of food in one big bowl | The cat can look after itself for a week | Lock it in the shed with a bucket of water and some biscuits
exercise: Why does a dog need a walk every day? | To stay fit and happy | Only so it can go to the toilet | To get tired so it never barks | Dogs only need walks at weekends
exercise: Why are hunting games good for a cat? | They keep its mind busy and happy | They teach it to hunt birds outside | They make it eat less food each day | They stop it from sleeping all day
exercise: Why do people say a puppy is for life, not just for Christmas? | Puppies grow up and need care for many years | Puppies only live until the next Christmas | Puppies can be given back after the holidays | Puppies stay small and cute for ever
exercise: Which of these foods is dangerous for dogs? | Raisins | Plain cooked chicken | Raw carrots | Fresh water
exercise: What should you do if you think a pet has eaten something poisonous? | Tell an adult at once so they call a vet | Wait a week and see what happens | Give it chocolate to make it feel better | Make it run around to shake it off
test: «it's the dog's birthday, give him a choc bar as a treat yeah?» | No, chocolate is poisonous to dogs | Yes, dogs love chocolate and it is fine | Yes, but only white chocolate | No, because it will make him fat
test: In a cartoon a fat orange cat eats a whole tray of lasagne every day. What is true about real cats? | They need proper cat food; rich human food can make them ill | They need lasagne to stay healthy | They only eat vegetables | They can eat anything people eat, as long as it is cooked and warm
test: «mum got lilies for the table, the cat's sniffing them, it's fine yeah» What is right? | No, lilies are very dangerous to cats, even the pollen | Yes, cats only sniff and never chew | Yes, lilies are good for cats' fur and make it shine | No, but only the leaves are bad
test: A lost dog is found two towns away. How can a vet find its owner quickly? | Scan its microchip to find the number linked to the owner | Ask the dog where it lives | Look at the colour of its collar | Wait for the dog to walk home by itself, following its nose
test: «i want a puppy for xmas!! i'll walk it like twice, then nan can» What is the problem? | A dog needs walks and care every day for many years | Nothing, dogs only need walks at Christmas | Puppies cannot go outside until they are ten years old | Nans are not allowed to walk dogs
test: Count them: a family has 2 dogs and 3 cats. How many bowls of fresh water are needed if each pet has its own? | 5 | 2 | 3 | 6
test: What would happen if a hamster's dirty bedding were left for a whole month? | It would smell and the hamster could get ill | Nothing, hamsters clean their own cages | The hamster would make new bedding itself | The old bedding would slowly turn into good soil for the plants
test: Count them: the family's 2 dogs each need 2 walks a day. How many walks is that in a week? | 28 | 14 | 4 | 21
test: «we're off to Spain for 2 weeks, just leave the bunny loads of food, sorted» What is right? | No, someone must feed it and check on it every day | Yes, rabbits only eat once a week | Yes, rabbits sleep the whole time you are away | No, rabbits must come on holiday too, on the plane with you
test: «cat's bored, just sits there all day, so what» What could help? | Play hunting games with toys | Give it more food so it sleeps | Shut it in a smaller room alone | Nothing, cats are never bored
test: In a famous cartoon a beagle sleeps on top of his kennel every night. What does a real dog need? | A safe, warm, comfortable place to sleep | Only the roof of a kennel, like the cartoon | No bed at all, just the cold garden at night | A small cage that is cleaned out once a year
exam: Identify the principal reason for which the feeding of chocolate to dogs is to be avoided. | It contains a substance which the dog is unable to break down efficiently and which is poisonous | It causes the immediate loss of the dog's coat | It is invariably refused by the dog on account of its taste | It induces a permanent loss of memory in the dog
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the hazard presented by lilies to domestic cats? | All parts, including pollen ingested during grooming, may cause damage to the kidneys | Only the scent is harmful, causing mild sneezing | Only the petals are harmful, and only when consumed in considerable quantity by the animal | Lilies present no hazard whatsoever to domestic cats
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the function of a microchip implanted in a pet. | It carries a number linked to the owner, permitting the return of a lost animal | It transmits the continuous location of the animal by satellite | It confers immunity against the principal infectious diseases of domestic animals | It regulates the appetite of the animal
exam: Ascertain the principle expressed by the statement that a pet is for life and not solely for a festive occasion. | The owner assumes responsibility for the care of the animal throughout its lifespan | The animal may be returned to the vendor after the festive period | The animal is expected to survive only until the following year | The animal requires attention only during the festive period
